 "drivingQuestion": "Why is one liable for offering portions on top of meat outside if the meat interposes?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "answered in the next unit",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "zevachim.110a.13",
   "daf": "110a",
   "sentenceId": 12,
   "he": "הַמַּקְרִיב קָדָשִׁים. אַמַּאי? וְהָאִיכָּא חֲצִיצָה!",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: «One who sacrifices sacrificial» meat, which is eaten, and sacrificial portions, i.e., those that are to be burned on the altar, outside the courtyard, is liable for the sacrifice of the sacrificial portions, but not for the meat. The Gemara asks: «Why» is he liable? If the meat is placed directly on the altar’s fire and then the sacrificial portions are placed upon the meat, «isn’t there an interposition» between the altar and the sacrificial portions? Since if they were offered in the Temple in that manner, one would not have fulfilled the obligation, one should not be liable if he offers them up in this manner outside the Temple."
  }
 ],
